// ============================================================================
// Hunt: Windows "SignDrop" CLFS privilege-escalation zero-day
//   (researcher Abdelhamid Naceri / Nightmare Eclipse, PoC published
//   November 10, 2026 - the same day as Microsoft's November Patch Tuesday)
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1068 (Privilege Escalation), T1059.003 (Windows Command
//   Shell - the end state is a SYSTEM shell)
// Data: Microsoft Defender DeviceFileEvents, DeviceProcessEvents
//   (Defender for Endpoint)
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-14-2026.html
// Logic: SignDrop targets the Common Log File System (CLFS) driver and
//   abuses the very mitigation Microsoft introduced in October 2025 to
//   defend CLFS against authentication attacks. A standard user prepares
//   a CLFS log and container, then uses Object Manager symbolic links to
//   redirect where the driver's .cnpf authentication file lands. When a
//   privileged process such as Windows Defender later opens the prepared
//   log - coerced through its signature-update RPC interface - CLFS
//   creates the authentication file in that process's SYSTEM context, at
//   the attacker-chosen path, with the original file's access
//   permissions. The result is a SYSTEM-owned file the standard user can
//   write to, and a SYSTEM shell. Naceri tested the exploit on Windows
//   11 26H2, an experimental 26H2 build, and an experimental Windows
//   Server 2025 build. There is no CVE, no patch, and no reported
//   in-the-wild exploitation, and the flaw is not in CISA's KEV catalog
//   - report the capability as demonstrated, the exposure as estimated,
//   and the exploitation status as unproven. The researcher's estimate
//   that about 80 percent of Windows installations are vulnerable is his
//   own, not a confirmed count; independent reproduction by Will Dormann
//   confirmed the exploit works as described, though not on all Windows
//   11 versions, and Dormann's qualifier is that the vulnerable machines
//   appear to be Windows 11 26H2 installations that have existed for
//   more than 90 days, which narrows the real population considerably.
//   Microsoft has not commented. Naceri notes there is currently no
//   solution except waiting for a patch: disabling the CLFS mitigation
//   would re-expose machines to the memory-corruption attacks it was
//   built to stop. Scope check (manual, not a query): read the CLFS
//   authentication mode in the registry - a value of 0 or 1 (learning or
//   enforcement mode) means the machine is in scope. No IoCs are
//   invented here: no hashes, paths, or process names beyond the ones
//   the brief itself names (.cnpf, Windows Defender, CLFS).
//   Any hit on Variant 1 or 2 means a prepared CLFS log was opened by a
//   privileged process - treat the host as potentially escalated and
//   look for the SYSTEM shell in Variant 3.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: a CLFS .cnpf authentication file created in a user-writable path --
// The .cnpf file is created by CLFS in the SYSTEM context of the
// privileged process that opened the prepared log - but at the path the
// attacker chose through the symbolic-link redirect. A .cnpf landing in
// a user-controlled folder (profile, Temp, Public, Desktop, Downloads)
// is not normal CLFS behaviour and is the core artifact of this
// technique. Scoped to 14 days from the November 10 disclosure.
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(14d)
| where FileName endswith ".cnpf"
| where FolderPath has_any (@"\Users\", @"\Temp\", @"\Public\", @"\Desktop\", @"\Downloads\")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, ActionType, FileName, FolderPath, SHA256, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessAccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 2: Windows Defender creating or opening a .cnpf file --
// The trigger step is a privileged process opening the attacker-prepared
// log. The brief names Windows Defender, coerced through its
// signature-update RPC interface, as the privileged process. Defender
// (MsMpEng.exe, with MpCmdRun.exe for signature-update work) has no
// ordinary reason to touch a .cnpf file, so any such event is a strong
// signal. Triage against Variant 1: the same host, the same .cnpf path,
// and a standard-user process that prepared the log shortly before.
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(14d)
| where FileName endswith ".cnpf"
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("MsMpEng.exe", "MpCmdRun.exe")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, ActionType, FileName, FolderPath, SHA256, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessAccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 3: a shell running as SYSTEM but launched by a non-SYSTEM process --
// The end state of SignDrop is a SYSTEM shell for a standard user. A
// command shell or script host running under the SYSTEM account whose
// parent process runs as a normal user account is the tell. Legitimate
// SYSTEM shells are launched by service hosts, so those parents are
// excluded; anything left is worth a look, especially on a host that
// also appears in Variant 1 or 2. Scoped to 14 days from disclosure.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(14d)
| where FileName in~ ("cmd.exe", "powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe", "wscript.exe", "cscript.exe")
| where AccountName endswith "SYSTEM"
| where InitiatingProcessAccountName !endswith "SYSTEM"
| where InitiatingProcessFileName !in~ ("services.exe", "svchost.exe", "wininit.exe", "taskhostw.exe", "smss.exe", "csrss.exe")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, AccountName, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessAccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
